      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To solve for the volume of the frustum, we start by calculating the volume of the original pyramid and then the volumes of both the smaller pyramid and the frustum. The volume \\( V \\) of a pyramid is given by the formula:\n\n\\[\nV = \\frac{1}{3} \\times \\text{Base Area} \\times \\text{Height}\n\\]\n\nFirst, we calculate the volume of the original pyramid. The base edge of the original pyramid is 12 cm and its altitude is 8 cm. The base area of the original pyramid is:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Base Area} = 12^2 = 144 \\text{ cm}^2\n\\]\n\nSo, the volume of the original pyramid is:\n\n\\[\nV_{\\text{original}} = \\frac{1}{3} \\times 144 \\times 8 = \\frac{1}{3} \\times 1152 = 384 \\text{ cm}^3\n\\]\n\nNext, we calculate the volume of the smaller pyramid. The base edge of the smaller pyramid is 6 cm and its altitude is 4 cm. The base area of the smaller pyramid is:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Base Area} = 6^2 = 36 \\text{ cm}^2\n\\]\n\nSo, the volume of the smaller pyramid is:\n\n\\[\nV_{\\text{small}} = \\frac{1}{3} \\times 36 \\times 4 = \\frac{1}{3} \\times 144 = 48 \\text{ cm}^3\n\\]\n\nNow, we can find the volume of the frustum by subtracting the volume of the smaller pyramid from the volume of the original pyramid:\n\n\\[\nV_{\\text{frustum}} = V_{\\text{original}} - V_{\\text{small}} = 384 - 48 = 336 \\text{ cm}^3\n\\]\n\nThus, the volume of the frustum is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{336}\n\\]",
